WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.643
The biggest idea in this lesson is that the resultant force tells you about change in motion, not motion already in progress.

2
00:00:05.643 --> 00:00:11.286
A craft can be moving and still have a zero Master Arrow.

3
00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:16.929
A craft can also have a non-zero Master Arrow while still moving the other way for a while.

4
00:00:16.929 --> 00:00:22.572
That is why force state and motion state have to be kept separate.

5
00:00:22.572 --> 00:00:28.215
Newton's first law becomes much calmer once the learner stops trying to read velocity directly from the force arrows.

6
00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:33.858
Keep this takeaway in view: Resultant force tells the next change in motion, not the motion that already exists.

7
00:00:33.858 --> 00:00:39.501
Force state means Master Arrow.

8
00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:45.144
The Master Arrow is the resultant force after the whole force picture is combined.

9
00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:50.787
Motion state means current velocity.

10
00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:56.430
This tells how the craft is moving right now before the next change happens.

11
00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:02.510
This diagram does an important job.

12
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:08.590
On one side, equal and opposite forces are both present, but the Master Arrow is zero because they cancel.

13
00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:14.670
On the other side, no force arrows act at that moment, and the Master Arrow is also zero.

14
00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:20.750
Those two pictures are physically different, yet the acceleration outcome is the same: zero.

15
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:26.830
That is the lesson gain.

16
00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:32.910
Zero resultant force means no acceleration.

17
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:38.990
It does not tell you whether the object is at rest or already cruising steadily.

18
00:01:38.990 --> 00:01:45.070
Keep this takeaway in view: Balanced forces and no forces are different force stories that can share the same zero-acceleration result.

19
00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:51.150
Balanced arrows: Forces are present, but they cancel to give zero Master Arrow.

20
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:57.230
No arrows: No forces act now, yet zero Master Arrow still means zero acceleration.

21
00:01:57.230 --> 00:02:02.416
A useful analogy is a pilot display.

22
00:02:02.416 --> 00:02:07.602
Many thruster arrows may appear around the craft, but the pilot needs one summary steering instruction.

23
00:02:07.602 --> 00:02:12.788
That summary is the Master Arrow.

24
00:02:12.788 --> 00:02:17.974
The ship responds to that net instruction, not to one loud-looking arrow taken on its own.

25
00:02:17.974 --> 00:02:23.160
This analogy matters because it teaches the learner to collapse the whole force picture before trying to predict the acceleration.

26
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.346
Keep this takeaway in view: The craft responds to the summary force picture, not to an uncombined list of arrows.

27
00:02:28.346 --> 00:02:33.532
Many arrows means individual forces.

28
00:02:33.532 --> 00:02:38.718
These are the separate pushes and pulls acting on the craft.

29
00:02:38.718 --> 00:02:43.904
One summary arrow means resultant force.

30
00:02:43.904 --> 00:02:49.090
This is the net force that actually sets the acceleration.

31
00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:53.484
The method is simple but disciplined.

32
00:02:53.484 --> 00:02:57.878
Add forces that act in the same direction.

33
00:02:57.878 --> 00:03:02.272
Subtract opposite-direction forces that act along the same line, then keep the direction of the larger side.

34
00:03:02.272 --> 00:03:06.667
That leftover force is the Master Arrow.

35
00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:11.061
Only after that should the learner talk about acceleration.

36
00:03:11.061 --> 00:03:15.455
This is what stops the force picture from becoming a list of disconnected numbers.

37
00:03:15.455 --> 00:03:19.849
Keep this takeaway in view: Force combination comes before acceleration language.

38
00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:24.243
Add: Same-direction arrows support one another.

39
00:03:24.243 --> 00:03:28.637
Subtract: Opposite-direction arrows compete along the same axis.

40
00:03:28.637 --> 00:03:33.032
same direction -> add.

41
00:03:33.032 --> 00:03:37.426
opposite directions -> subtract.

42
00:03:37.426 --> 00:03:41.820
The leftover same-line force is the Master Arrow, or resultant force.

43
00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.858
Suppose a craft is moving west but the Master Arrow points east.

44
00:03:46.858 --> 00:03:51.896
The craft does not teleport into eastward motion immediately.

45
00:03:51.896 --> 00:03:56.935
Instead, the eastward acceleration first reduces the westward velocity.

46
00:03:56.935 --> 00:04:01.973
Only later, if that acceleration continues long enough, will the craft stop and then reverse.

47
00:04:01.973 --> 00:04:07.011
That is why present velocity and next acceleration must be held in separate boxes.

48
00:04:07.011 --> 00:04:12.049
Motion already happening and motion change now happening are different stories.

49
00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:17.087
Keep this takeaway in view: Acceleration can oppose the current motion without instantly replacing it.

50
00:04:17.087 --> 00:04:22.125
Current velocity means west.

51
00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:27.164
This is the motion the craft already has.

52
00:04:27.164 --> 00:04:32.202
Master Arrow means east.

53
00:04:32.202 --> 00:04:37.240
This sets an eastward acceleration right now.

54
00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:42.566
A common trap is to see zero resultant force and announce that the object must be stationary.

55
00:04:42.566 --> 00:04:47.891
That is wrong.

56
00:04:47.891 --> 00:04:53.217
Zero resultant force means zero acceleration, so the object can remain at rest or keep moving with constant velocity.

57
00:04:53.217 --> 00:04:58.542
Another trap is to say balanced forces means no forces.

58
00:04:58.542 --> 00:05:03.868
Balanced forces are real forces whose effects cancel.

59
00:05:03.868 --> 00:05:09.193
The distinction matters because it keeps the force story honest.

60
00:05:09.193 --> 00:05:14.519
Keep this takeaway in view: Zero resultant force removes acceleration, not necessarily motion.

61
00:05:14.519 --> 00:05:19.844
Zero Master Arrow means zero acceleration.

62
00:05:19.844 --> 00:05:25.170
Zero acceleration can mean rest or steady velocity.

63
00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.307
Check yourself.

64
00:05:28.307 --> 00:05:31.443
What does the Master Arrow tell you?

65
00:05:31.443 --> 00:05:34.580
The acceleration direction and whether it is zero or non-zero.

66
00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.717
What does the current velocity tell you?

67
00:05:37.717 --> 00:05:40.853
How the craft is moving at that instant.

68
00:05:40.853 --> 00:05:43.990
Can a craft move steadily with zero Master Arrow?

69
00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.127
Yes.

70
00:05:47.127 --> 00:05:50.263
Can a craft accelerate east while still moving west?

71
00:05:50.263 --> 00:05:53.400
Also yes.

72
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.537
Those are the three core checks of the lesson.

73
00:05:56.537 --> 00:05:59.673
Keep this takeaway in view: Force state and motion state must stay separate all the way through the lesson.

74
00:05:59.673 --> 00:06:02.810
Master Arrow means acceleration story.

75
00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.947
This is the resultant-force idea.

76
00:06:05.947 --> 00:06:09.083
Velocity means current motion story.

77
00:06:09.083 --> 00:06:12.220
This is what the craft is doing right now.

78
00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:18.094
You are ready for the worked example now.

79
00:06:18.094 --> 00:06:23.967
The craft is moving west at three metres per second while fourteen newtons east and nine newtons west act on it.

80
00:06:23.967 --> 00:06:29.841
Combine the forces first: fourteen east minus nine west leaves five newtons east.

81
00:06:29.841 --> 00:06:35.715
That is the Master Arrow, so the acceleration points east.

82
00:06:35.715 --> 00:06:41.588
But keep the current motion separate: the craft is still moving west right now.

83
00:06:41.588 --> 00:06:47.462
So the immediate motion change is that it slows down while still travelling west.

84
00:06:47.462 --> 00:06:53.335
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: combine the arrows, then compare acceleration with current motion.

85
00:06:53.335 --> 00:06:59.209
Step 1: Combine opposite same-line forces to find the Master Arrow.

86
00:06:59.209 --> 00:07:05.083
Step 2: Use the Master Arrow to state the acceleration direction.

87
00:07:05.083 --> 00:07:10.956
Master Arrow = 14 east - 9 west = 5 N east.

88
00:07:10.956 --> 00:07:16.830
Force combination and motion interpretation happen in that order.
